.TH "QSHARE" "1" "2026-11-12" "passerelle 0.1.0" "Passerelle host tools"
.SH NAME
qshare \- Share a host directory with a QNX guest under QEMU
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qshare
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.RI [ \fIDIRECTORY\fR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Share a host directory with a QNX guest under QEMU (DX\-01)
.P
qshare prints the exact QEMU command\-line options that share a host directory
with a QNX 8.0 guest over 9P2000.L or virtiofs, and can start QEMU (and
virtiofsd) itself with \-\-run. It also emits the mkqnximage snippets that
install the guest driver, writes the CMake and Rust example projects with
\-\-example, and replays the whole demonstration with \-\-demo.
.P
Inside the guest, the share is mounted with the passerelle driver at the
\-\-mount\-point location; qrun(1) then runs programs from it, and fsproof(1)
and pbench(1) prove and measure it.
.P
Three subcommands answer the everyday questions: qshare doctor says what is
missing on this machine, qshare init scaffolds a guest project ready to be
shared, and qshare smoke proves the whole chain here (configure, build, ctest,
pytest, the reference benches and the guest check).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
show this help message and exit
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
print the version and exit
.TP
\fIDIRECTORY\fR
host directory to share
.TP
\fB\-\-tag\fR \fITAG\fR, \fB\-t\fR \fITAG\fR
mount tag (9P <=31 bytes, virtiofs <=36) (default: configuration 'tag' =
'hostshare')
.TP
\fB\-\-proto\fR \fIPROTO\fR
share protocol (default: configuration 'proto' = '9p') (choices: 9p, virtiofs)
.TP
\fB\-\-security\fR \fISECURITY\fR, \fB\-S\fR \fISECURITY\fR
QEMU security model, no root needed (default: configuration 'security' =
'mapped\-xattr') (choices: mapped\-xattr, mapped\-file, passthrough, none)
.TP
\fB\-\-mount\-point\fR \fIMOUNT_POINT\fR
guest mount point (default: configuration 'mount\-point' = '/host')
.TP
\fB\-\-driver\fR \fIDRIVER\fR
guest driver name (default: configuration 'driver' = 'passerelle')
.TP
\fB\-\-driver\-bin\fR \fIDRIVER_BIN\fR
absolute host path of the driver binary (default: configuration 'driver\-bin'
= '/opt/passerelle/sbin/passerelle')
.TP
\fB\-\-emit\fR
print one shell\-ready option line
.TP
\fB\-\-emit\-image\fR
print the mkqnximage snippets
.TP
\fB\-\-mkqnximage\fR \fIVM_DIR\fR
write snippets into <VM_DIR>/local/snippets
.TP
\fB\-\-ifs\fR
use the IFS layout for the snippets
.TP
\fB\-\-run\fR \fIIMAGE\fR
start QEMU on IMAGE (kernel or disk)
.TP
\fB\-\-arch\fR \fIARCH\fR
guest architecture for \-\-run (default: configuration 'arch' = 'x86_64')
.TP
\fB\-\-qemu\-arg\fR \fIARG\fR
extra QEMU argument, repeatable (appended after the share options). Used for a
direct\-kernel boot, e.g. \-\-qemu\-arg=\-initrd
\-\-qemu\-arg=/tmp/initramfs.cpio.gz
.TP
\fB\-\-wait\-for\fR \fIMARKER\fR
consider the run successful when MARKER appears on the console
.TP
\fB\-\-console\-log\fR \fIFILE\fR
write the guest console here (default: /tmp/pgw\-qemu\-console.log)
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fITIMEOUT\fR
seconds to wait for the console marker (default: configuration 'timeout' =
'120')
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\fR \fILOG\fR
write the 9P trace to LOG (pbench roundtrips)
.TP
\fB\-\-demo\fR
replay the demonstration (DX\-02)
.TP
\fB\-\-example\fR \fIEXAMPLE\fR
write the CMake/Rust example projects into the share (DX\-04) (choices: cmake,
rust, all)
.TP
\fB\-\-security\-help\fR
explain the security models (SEC\-03)
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-config\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
print the effective configuration (text or json) and exit (choices: text,
json)
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
success (options printed, snippets written, run or demo finished)
.TP
\fB2\fR
usage error, or invalid input (missing directory, bad tag, ...)
.TP
\fB3\fR
host environment error (QEMU or virtiofsd missing, run failed)
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI<VM_DIR>/local/snippets/\fR
mkqnximage fragments written by \-\-mkqnximage
.TP
\fI<share>/cmake_example/, <share>/rust_example/\fR
projects written by \-\-example
.TP
\fI<share>/README.md, <share>/qemu\-options.txt, <share>/guest\-setup.txt\fR
the project written by 'qshare init'
.TP
\fI/tmp/pgw\-qemu\-console.log\fR
default guest console log of \-\-run
.SH SEE ALSO
qrun(1), fsproof(1), pbench(1), qemu(1), virtiofsd(1)
